WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.880
Disney's Peter Pan has been loved by&nbsp;
families for generations. Unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:09.200
this animated classic hasn't aged&nbsp;
entirely well over the years. Much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:14.080
of this goes over kids' heads, but older&nbsp;
viewers might be surprised by some scenes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:17.840
For good or bad, here are things&nbsp;
that only adults notice in Peter Pan.

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:23.760
As a child, it feels perfectly innocent to&nbsp;
fantasize about some magical being coming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:29.200
to your window to take you away on adventures in&nbsp;
faraway lands. As an adult, however, the thought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:34.400
of strange boys visiting underage daughters&nbsp;
via bedroom windows is much less charming.

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:38.640
When Wendy claims Peter Pan has been to the&nbsp;
nursery before and must be surely coming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:43.520
again because she'd found his shadow, her&nbsp;
mother seems a bit perplexed. Her father,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:47.840
on the other hand, brushes it off as childish&nbsp;
fiddle-faddle and mocks his wife's concern.

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:50.880
"Goodness gracious. Whatever shall we do?"

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:56.160
However, had the same conversation been&nbsp;
with more literal or protective guardians,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.160 --> 00:01:01.680
Peter likely would've found a father waiting with&nbsp;
his shotgun in that nursery instead of a pining,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:06.160
unattended Wendy. At the very least, it&nbsp;
would've been wise to leave the family dog,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:09.680
Nana, in the house with the kids,&nbsp;
rather than tied up outside.

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:14.480
On top of that, the grown mind can't help&nbsp;
but question Mr. and Mrs. Darling's decision&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:19.200
to leave their young children home alone for&nbsp;
hours without any sort of adult supervision.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:20.960
If you're not going to have your dog protect your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:24.640
children while you're out partying,&nbsp;
at least find a human to babysit.

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:30.000
Fairies are some of the most iconic creatures&nbsp;
in the worlds of fantasy, but their disposition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:35.600
varies widely between sources. In some instances,&nbsp;
they're benevolent and wise, like Cinderella's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:40.240
Fairy Godmother, while other portrayals of&nbsp;
the magical beings show a much darker and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:45.280
malevolent nature. Peter Pan's sidekick, Tinker&nbsp;
Bell, seems to lie somewhere in the middle.

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:49.840
Tink displays a mean, jealous streak the moment&nbsp;
she learns Wendy will be accompanying them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:54.880
back to Neverland, going so far as to make an&nbsp;
attempt on her life without Peter's knowledge.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.680 --> 00:02:00.480
After abandoning Wendy while flying to Peter's&nbsp;
hideout, Hangman's Tree, Tinker Bell tells&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:05.360
the awaiting Lost Boys that he gave orders to&nbsp;
shoot down a terrible "Wendy-bird" spotted in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:10.400
the skies. Sadly, the Lost Boys follow Tinker&nbsp;
Bell's instructions and attack her, but Peter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:16.080
manages to fly over just in time to save Wendy&nbsp;
from crashing down onto the jagged rocks below.

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:19.680
When Peter asks Tinker Bell if she's&nbsp;
guilty or not guilty of attempting to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:23.600
get Wendy shot down, she has an&nbsp;
honest and disturbing response.

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:27.600
"Guilty? But don't ya know you might killed her?"

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:34.880
After being banished for her malicious&nbsp;
plot, Tink makes things even worse by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:39.680
turning on Peter — disclosing his hideout to&nbsp;
his arch-nemesis, Captain Hook. Thankfully,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:44.400
she redeems herself in the end by saving Peter&nbsp;
from a bomb that Hook disguises as a gift,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:48.880
but still, it doesn't excuse Tink's&nbsp;
attempts to plant Wendy six feet under.

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:54.240
Being a child forever sounds pretty liberating&nbsp;
on the surface, but when examined more deeply,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:59.040
it definitely has a dark side. While&nbsp;
infantile behavior brings carefree fun&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:03.920
and spontaneity, it can also cultivate&nbsp;
a short-sighted and selfish nature.

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:06.480
This is exhibited time and again by Peter Pan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:10.720
who repeatedly puts others in danger&nbsp;
thanks to his immature actions and motives.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:15.040
From the moment he's introduced, he&nbsp;
constantly makes everything about himself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:19.760
As a kid, it's easy to mistake Peter's decision&nbsp;
to take Wendy and her brothers to Neverland as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:24.800
a form of "liberation" and a heroic rescue from&nbsp;
growing up — which George tells Wendy she'll have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:29.920
to do starting the next day by moving out of the&nbsp;
nursery with her brothers and into her own room.

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:33.200
However, more mature viewers are apt&nbsp;
to note that Peter only invites them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:36.720
to Neverland because Wendy's imminent&nbsp;
relocation would put an end to her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:41.120
adventure stories. And Peter truly loves&nbsp;
these tales because they're about him.

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:46.160
But the character's dark side doesn't stop there.&nbsp;
Peter also treats all the females in his life like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:50.080
trinkets rather than people, quickly moving&nbsp;
on to the next shiny thing in front of him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:53.520
like when he leaves Wendy behind&nbsp;
for Tiger Lily. To top it off,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:58.720
he occasionally drops misogynistic insights&nbsp;
and demands that haven't aged well at all.

00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:00.320
"Girls talk too much."

00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:03.760
"Yes, girls talk too...Hmm? Oh."

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:05.440
"Well, get on with it girl."

00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:07.200
"My name is Wendy."

00:04:07.840 --> 00:04:12.240
When watching Peter Pan, adult viewers can't&nbsp;
help but notice disturbing levels of racism in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:17.680
the classic film. It's so bad that Disney+&nbsp;
even added a disclaimer before the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.680 --> 00:04:22.880
warning viewers about, quote, "negative depictions&nbsp;
and-or mistreatment of people or cultures."

00:04:22.880 --> 00:04:27.920
Not only does this film depict Native Americans&nbsp;
in horrifically prejudiced ways, the movie is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.920 --> 00:04:32.880
carpet-bombed with racist slurs against them.&nbsp;
Everyone, from innocent children and the heroic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:38.160
Pan to the villainous Captain Hook, casually&nbsp;
speak of them as "savages" and much worse. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:43.360
poor treatment of Native Americans and other First&nbsp;
Nations people culminates in a singalong titled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:49.040
"What Makes the Red Man Red," which is loaded with&nbsp;
cultural appropriation and racist stereotypes.

00:04:49.760 --> 00:04:54.320
Early in the film, we learn that Captain Hook&nbsp;
has a perfectly viable reason for being so upset&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.320 --> 00:04:59.040
with Peter Pan. After all, he's responsible&nbsp;
for Hook having a hook in the first place.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:03.840
During a skirmish with the villain, Peter cut&nbsp;
off Hook's hand and fed it to a nearby crocodile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:08.320
who then developed a taste for the pirate&nbsp;
leader's flesh. As a result, the crocodile&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:13.120
constantly hunts Captain Hook and follows him&nbsp;
around Neverland hoping for another nibble.

00:05:13.120 --> 00:05:18.160
Luckily, the crocodile also swallowed a clock,&nbsp;
so he can be easily detected by the distinctive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:22.880
sound of ticking emanating from his tummy. Hook's&nbsp;
encounters with the crocodile make for some of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.880 --> 00:05:27.840
funniest moments in the film, but it also stirs&nbsp;
up questions within the discerning adult mind.

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:32.480
Specifically, one can't help but wonder&nbsp;
why Hook doesn't just organize a group of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.480 --> 00:05:37.120
his men to execute the beast so he no&nbsp;
longer has to live in perpetual fear.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.120 --> 00:05:42.480
He's a badass pirate captain, in charge of a&nbsp;
ship loaded with dangerous men, cannons and other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.480 --> 00:05:47.040
weapons — he should put those to good use against&nbsp;
the crocodile so he can move on with his life.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:52.160
Plus, the croc doesn't seem to be too elusive&nbsp;
or quick moving. However, like most kids, adults&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.160 --> 00:05:56.560
should overlook this leap in logic and enjoy the&nbsp;
timeless comedy of these two fighting it out.

00:05:57.280 --> 00:06:02.240
An uncomfortable aspect of Peter Pan comes into&nbsp;
focus when analyzing the questionable relationship&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:06.880
between Peter and Wendy. The two technically&nbsp;
meet for the first time after he breaks into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.880 --> 00:06:11.280
the nursery window at night while her parents&nbsp;
are out, which is unnerving enough. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:15.200
Peter had to have been there at least once&nbsp;
before while she was sleeping, considering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:19.840
Wendy found his misplaced shadow in the nursery&nbsp;
— which definitely amps up the creep factor.

00:06:20.400 --> 00:06:25.120
Then things get even more uncomfortable. While&nbsp;
considering the weighty decision of whether she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.120 --> 00:06:30.400
should go with a stranger to a strange land, Wendy&nbsp;
wonders aloud what her mother will think. Peter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.400 --> 00:06:35.280
being unwise in the ways of the world, asks&nbsp;
Wendy what a mother is, to which she replies...

00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:40.400
"A mother is someone who loves and&nbsp;
cares for you and tells you stories."

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:42.080
"Good! You can be our mother."

00:06:42.720 --> 00:06:47.360
Moments after this statement, Wendy declares she's&nbsp;
so excited to see Peter that she's going to give&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.360 --> 00:06:53.360
him a kiss — and not in the motherly sort of way.&nbsp;
This has some weird Freudian connotations to say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.360 --> 00:06:57.760
the least, and it sets a bit of an odd undertone&nbsp;
between the two throughout the rest of the film.

00:06:58.480 --> 00:07:03.040
In the latter half of the movie, Wendy sings&nbsp;
a touching song called "Your Mother and Mine."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.040 --> 00:07:07.360
It's a sweet melody praising motherly love,&nbsp;
soothing enough to lull her rowdy brothers and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.360 --> 00:07:13.200
the Lost Boys into a relaxed, blissful state.&nbsp;
Wendy's song even brings the pirates to tears.

00:07:13.200 --> 00:07:18.560
One pirate in particular, Mr. Smee, Captain Hook's&nbsp;
comical boatswain, is shown crying while pulling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.560 --> 00:07:22.800
up his shirt to reveal a classic "Mom" tattoo&nbsp;
on his chest, making this one of the film's more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.800 --> 00:07:27.920
memorable and humorous moments. Even tough guys&nbsp;
love their mommas, and when watching this scene as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.920 --> 00:07:32.960
an adult, it genuinely pulls on the heartstrings&nbsp;
and makes Smee's actions far more relatable.

00:07:33.520 --> 00:07:37.920
As a kid, it's easy to take songs about mothers&nbsp;
for granted, but once you've moved out on your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.920 --> 00:07:42.240
own, your mom isn't as easily accessible,&nbsp;
and distance makes the heart grow fonder.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.800 --> 00:07:46.240
More than a few adults watching this&nbsp;
movie probably found themselves getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.240 --> 00:07:49.120
misty during this touching&nbsp;
tribute to mothers everywhere.

00:07:50.000 --> 00:07:52.080
They say you should never meet your heroes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.080 --> 00:07:57.120
and for those who idolize mermaids, this is&nbsp;
particularly true while watching Peter Pan.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.120 --> 00:08:00.160
Mermaids are generally depicted&nbsp;
as friendly, benevolent creatures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.160 --> 00:08:05.360
especially in such films as Splash or The Little&nbsp;
Mermaid. However, Neverland's resident merfolk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.360 --> 00:08:10.560
have darker natures, which Wendy learns to her&nbsp;
detriment while exploring the island with Peter.

00:08:10.560 --> 00:08:15.760
As it turns out, Peter is quite the ladies' man,&nbsp;
and Wendy finds herself vying for his attention&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.760 --> 00:08:20.560
while he catches up with fan-girling mermaids&nbsp;
in a Neverland lagoon. Once they notice Wendy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.560 --> 00:08:24.640
however, the mermaids switch from&nbsp;
flirtatious vixens to jealous tyrants.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.640 --> 00:08:28.000
While Peter looks on laughing, the mermaids&nbsp;
attempt to pull her under the water.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.560 --> 00:08:32.880
This is the second time Wendy nearly meets&nbsp;
her fate at the hands of one of Peter's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.880 --> 00:08:37.520
female friends, and like Tinker Bell, the&nbsp;
mermaids own up to their true intentions.

00:08:37.520 --> 00:08:43.040
In sociopathic-like fashion, the mermaids&nbsp;
acknowledge exactly what they were trying to do...

00:08:43.600 --> 00:08:45.680
"We were only trying to drown her."

00:08:46.720 --> 00:08:51.600
After losing his temper early in the film, George&nbsp;
Darling takes his frustrations out on Nana,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.600 --> 00:08:56.800
the canine nanny, by banishing the poor&nbsp;
dog outside to her lonely, drafty doghouse.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.520 --> 00:09:02.880
It's sadly ironic as she's the least chaotic&nbsp;
and most helpful member of the entire family.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.880 --> 00:09:08.480
This is extra cruel considering she's not just&nbsp;
any dog — Nana thinks of herself as a nursemaid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.480 --> 00:09:13.360
and that she belongs inside watching over the&nbsp;
children. She does all the things a traditional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.360 --> 00:09:18.240
caretaker would, modifying some activities&nbsp;
to accommodate her lack of opposable thumbs.

00:09:18.240 --> 00:09:22.880
It's hard not to marvel at her incredible&nbsp;
dexterity and ability to carry out a multitude&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.880 --> 00:09:27.600
of domestic tasks. Even professionally&nbsp;
trained dogs could learn a thing or two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.600 --> 00:09:32.960
from Nana's extensive skill set, and yet the&nbsp;
Darling family seems to take her entirely for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.960 --> 00:09:38.320
granted. For example, while George is angrily&nbsp;
pacing about, making a scene over his outfit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.320 --> 00:09:42.400
Nana continually tries to clean up and&nbsp;
care for the kids in the background.

00:09:42.400 --> 00:09:47.360
Nana is so loyal that when George struggles to&nbsp;
find the rope to tie her up outside, she pauses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.360 --> 00:09:52.560
mid-banishment to find it for him. While she's&nbsp;
just another humorous character for kids to enjoy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.560 --> 00:09:56.800
many adults watching the film probably wish&nbsp;
they could have their own Nana around the house.

00:09:57.840 --> 00:10:02.480
Although some of Peter Pan's content hasn't&nbsp;
aged too well, one of its spin-off movies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.480 --> 00:10:07.760
more than stands the test of time. Directed&nbsp;
by Steven Spielberg, 1991's Hook imagines&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.760 --> 00:10:12.160
a life where Peter falls in love with Wendy's&nbsp;
granddaughter, Moira, and leaves Neverland to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.160 --> 00:10:16.800
start a family of his own. It's a heartwarming&nbsp;
fantasy featuring a stellar cast — including&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.800 --> 00:10:22.080
Robin Williams as Peter Pan, Dustin Hoffman as&nbsp;
Captain Hook, and Julia Roberts as Tinker Bell.

00:10:22.080 --> 00:10:27.520
Ironically, the once carefree boy grows into an&nbsp;
incredibly stiff-necked workaholic with a fear of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.520 --> 00:10:33.520
heights. He's also too busy climbing the corporate&nbsp;
ladder to spend any quality time with his kids.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.520 --> 00:10:37.840
But daily life is interrupted for Peter's family&nbsp;
when Captain Hook kidnaps his children and takes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.840 --> 00:10:43.120
them back to Neverland. Peter is forced to face&nbsp;
his past in order to save his family's future.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.120 --> 00:10:46.320
In the process, he remembers&nbsp;
what's truly important in life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.320 --> 00:10:50.080
reconnects with his inner child, and&nbsp;
finds redemption with his loved ones.

00:10:50.720 --> 00:10:54.240
It's a grand adventure exploring&nbsp;
a boldly reimagined Neverland,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.240 --> 00:10:57.920
but it's also loaded with fun&nbsp;
callbacks to the 1953 classic.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.480 --> 00:11:03.120
Granted, critics weren't especially fond of it&nbsp;
when it first hit theaters, but nevertheless,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.120 --> 00:11:07.360
Hook is a joyous, family-friendly ride.&nbsp;
Plus, the special effects still hold up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.360 --> 00:11:11.520
the musical score is excellent, and best&nbsp;
of all, the movie doesn't contain any of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.520 --> 00:11:16.480
the outdated and cringy moments that only adults&nbsp;
would notice from the original animated version.

